<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herodias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 15 BCE–after 39 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herodias was a Herodian princess, a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>. She left her first husband, Herod's son Herod II, to marry his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a>, tetrarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Peraea</a>, a union denounced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>. When Antipas was exiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> in 39 CE, she chose to follow him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodias.jpg" alt="Herodias" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Delaroche · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%A9rodiade_-_Paul_Delaroche_-_Wallraf-Richartz-Museum_%26_Fondation_Corboud-6067_(without_frame).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Herodias was the daughter of Aristobulus IV and his wife Berenice, and a full sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis">Herod V</a>, king of Chalkis, and of Herod Agrippa, king of Judea. Herod the Great executed her father and his brother Alexander in 7 BCE and engaged her to Herod II, her half-uncle. Antipater II, Herod the Great's eldest son, opposed the marriage, but he was executed in 4 BCE for plotting to poison his father. Days before he died, Herod the Great divorced Herod II's mother Mariamne and dropped her son from the line of succession.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Herodias and Herod II had a daughter, Salome. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of Matthew and Mark name her husband Philip, but many scholars regard this as an error, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> names him Herod and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> drops the name Philip. Herodias later divorced him while he was alive, which Josephus says confounded the laws of the country, and she married his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a>.</p><p>Antipas divorced his first wife Phasaelis, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iv">Aretas IV</a> of Nabatea, in order to marry her. According to Matthew and Luke it was this proposed marriage that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> publicly criticised. The divorce also added a personal grievance to earlier disputes between Antipas and Aretas over territory on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Perea</a> and Nabatea. Aretas sent an army against him with a disastrous result for Antipas, and a Roman counter-offensive ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> was abandoned on the emperor's death in 37 CE. In 39 CE his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Agrippa I</a> accused Antipas of conspiracy against the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>, who exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Herodias accompanied him there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In Matthew and Mark, Herodias plays a major part in the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>: her daughter's dance before Antipas and his guests leads to her request for the Baptist's head. In Mark, Antipas does not wish to kill John, whom he liked to hear preach. Some biblical scholars question whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> give an accurate account, and some see in the struggle a recollection of the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a> and the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a>.</p><p>In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> her name became associated with beliefs about witches and the supernatural leader they joined at night. Medieval and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> artists often paired her with Salome in depictions of the power of women. Later works include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustave-flaubert">Gustave Flaubert</a>'s story Hérodias of 1877 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-wilde">Oscar Wilde</a>'s play Salomé.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herodias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His divorce of a daughter of the Nabataean king Aretas IV to marry <b>Herodias</b> led to a war he lost in 36.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-galilee-and-peraea">Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugdunum-convenarum">Lugdunum Convenarum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=20" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Herodias"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=20">What was here in 20 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/herod-antipas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Herod Antipas</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; c. 20 BCE–after 39 CE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
